From 7590b45f7996c2eba17f547c4a49eee5eef87850 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 10:28:18 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 25/26] VideoOutput: draw a depth video recorded sideways or
 upside down the right way up

The views of a picture with its depth map were drawn in the orientation the
frame is stored in, and the frame they were drawn into carried none of the
source frame's rotation or mirroring: a depth video with a rotation of 90,
180 or 270 degrees was shown unrotated, and the layer path was refused it.

Turn the picture and its depth map (and a second layer's) by the frame's
transformation, its surface's and its own, before libstereo-dibr draws the
views, so that the parallax runs along the width of the picture as it is
shown, and hand on a frame with nothing left to apply: no rotation, no
mirroring, the sample aspect ratio turned with a quarter turn. VideoOutput
takes the transformation from the frame it shows, so the output's own
orientation and mirroring still apply on top, and the compositor layer can
show the rotated depth video. A packed frame converted to RGBA to be drawn is
converted as stored, the transformation turning its parts.

tst_qquickvideostereo gains the tests: the rotated frames drawn into views
the right way up (a 90, 180 and 270 degree depth video, mirrored or not; YUV
4:4:4 and 4:2:0), the size the output shows them at, and the scene graph's
picture of each eye against the same video recorded the right way up.

